WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.660
It's no secret that 2016's Doctor Strange
is a part of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.731
but just how connected is the Master of the
Magic Arts to his fellow superheroes and his

00:00:08.731 --> 00:00:09.731
comic legacy?

00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:12.900
Here are a few Doctor Strange Easter eggs
you may not have noticed.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.570
New York, New York

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.420
The flow of time gets a little wacky in Doctor
Strange, thanks to the influence of the Eye

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.660
of Agamotto and the Infinity Stone powering
it.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.410
But we can safely say that the film takes
place in a world that's well aware of superheroes.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:29.020
During a few shots in the film, we see the
Avengers' tower standing tall over New York.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.069
However, by the end of Age of Ultron, the
Avengers have abandoned the tower and moved

00:00:33.069 --> 00:00:35.109
to upstate New York like Rhinebeck hipsters.

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:39.350
Either way, hero stuff is going on, so Strange
isn't alone.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.809
War Machine?

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:44.859
Moments before master surgeon Stephen Strange
has the car accident that changes his life,

00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:48.910
he hears about a 35-year-old with a spine
problem and an experimental suit of armor.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.149
And despite an Easter egg dropped in Daredevil,
it's probably not Stilt-Man.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:55.960
Mostly because no one would care if Stilt-Man
was paralyzed.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.600
Tony Stark made pretty sure that no one was
flying around using any of his designs by

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.290
blowing up his entire arsenal at the end of
Iron Man 3, and we know that poor ol' Rhodey

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.520
suffered a pretty devastating spinal injury
during Civil War.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.409
While the filmmakers have denied any connection,
how many people are really hanging out in

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.600
"experimental armor" in the MCU?

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.600
Shamballa

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:16.700
"What's this?

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.780
My mantra?"

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.119
"The WiFi password.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.899
We're not savages."

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:29.219
The concept of Shamballa isn't unique to Doctor
Strange, as the mystical, otherworldly kingdom

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:31.920
has existed in religious texts for centuries.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.090
Not coincidentally, Into Shamballa is also
the name of an important Doctor Strange story

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.229
from 1986, concerning not only the death of
the Ancient One, but also featuring Hamir,

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:43.759
the one-handed magic man from the temple in
the movie.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:47.640
While the comics reveal that Hamir is actually
Wong's father, the film flipped the script

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.570
in so many ways that it's not likely that
this relationship translated to the big screen.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.890
Drumm

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.640
Like most of Marvel's heroes have done at
one time or another, Doctor Strange has also

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.319
relinquished his title and let someone else
fly around in his Cloak of Levitation for

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:02.319
a while.

00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:05.969
In the film, the now-dead guardian of the
New York Sanctum was simply called "Drumm."

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:09.680
Eagle-eared Marvel fans will recognize the
name as belonging to brothers Jericho and

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.120
Daniel Drumm, the latter of which was a voodoo
houngan.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.730
Comic book things happen, and the two are
joined in spirit and become Brother Voodoo.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:20.980
At one point, Jericho Drumm takes possession
of the Eye of Agamotto and takes the name

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.340
"Doctor Voodoo," who also happens to be the
Sorcerer Supreme.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.940
It doesn't last forever, but it's clear that
the MCU is planting magical seeds everywhere.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.390
The Doctor Strange movie's prelude comics
confirm that this dead guardian was, in fact,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:34.390
Daniel Drumm.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.470
Not only that, but the comics — and a brief
moment in the film — also feature Tina Minoru

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.530
as another Master of the Mystic Arts.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.370
She's a secret practitioner of dark magic
in Marvel's Runaways, and mother of powerful

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.600
witch Nico Minoru.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.840
So, the Runaways have basically been confirmed
in the MCU, and psychic pet dinosaurs can't

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.060
be that far behind, which is the greatest
thing ever.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.620
The Living Tribunal

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.520
The hierarchy of power in Marvel Comics is
confusing, and even though there are only

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.560
a few big, bad guys in the MCU, it's already
reaching perplexing levels of power.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.340
Thanos is no slouch, but he's not much without
his Infinity Stones.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.290
Dormammu seems like a big deal, but he also
seems like a less-intelligent version of Galactus

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.290
so far.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:16.750
So when Mordo mentions the Staff of the Living
Tribunal, you know stuff is going to get crazy.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.290
"This is the staff of the Living Tribunal."

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.880
Though he's a bit obscure, the Living Tribunal
is one of Marvel's many godlike characters:

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.930
an enormous, all-powerful dude with a floating
head that has three faces.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:37.430
He essentially keeps all the multiverses in
balance and only has one being above him in

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:38.540
terms of power.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.310
No, actually it's this guy.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.020
And somehow, some part of The Living Tribunal
is captured in a wooden stick in the middle

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.120
of nowhere.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.120
Because magic!

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.500
The Wand of Watoomb and the Book of Vishanti

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:58.240
Doctor Strange has a few signature magical
items.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.660
Among them are the Eye of Agamotto, which
causes no end of trouble for Strange in both

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.460
the film and the comics, and the Cloak of
Levitation.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:10.400
Curiously missing is the Book of Vishanti,
which is basically Strange's bible and encyclopedia,

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.790
though it's probably locked up in Wong's well-guarded
library, right alongside the book that Strange

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.090
does use, the Book of Cagliostro.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.160
If you've been watching Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.,
you know that there's another magical book

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.400
floating around the MCU at the moment as well:
the Darkhold, which actually shares a few

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.310
pages with the Book of Cagliostro.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.440
Not to mention that the Book of Cagliostro
has a few pages that were actually written

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.630
by God.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.730
And probably a few by James Patterson.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.840
We also get a glimpse of one of Strange's
other signature weapons: the Wand of Watoomb.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.310
Even though it's Wong who wields the item
in defense of the Hong Kong Sanctum, that

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.780
weird, knobby, horned stick is unmistakable.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.500
Blu-ray replays will probably reveal even
more of Strange's armory locked up in the

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.630
Bleecker Street Sanctum.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.600
Which, by the way, is located at 177A, just
like in the comics.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.270
Nic West

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.510
The jealous, potato-chip-loving Doctor Nic
West is actually a character right out of

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:02.180
the pages of a 2007 Doctor Strange story called
"The Oath," where he plays the doctor who

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.960
bungles Strange's busted-up hands beyond usefulness.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.389
He begins magical training, just like Strange,
but he's a C student at best.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:13.760
Eventually, he accidentally kills someone
with magic, and he and Strange duke it out,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.100
with West falling off a building to his death.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.630
And that scene where an astral Strange helps
someone with an operation on his own mortal

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:20.630
body?

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.340
Straight out of "The Oath," too.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:23.490
Mindless Ones

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.670
The Dark Dimension basically has three main
inhabitants: Dormammu, his sister Umar, and…

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.840
"The mindless ones!"

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:36.470
Those guys are Dormammu's featureless, grey,
cycloptic hench-things.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:40.080
At the conclusion of Doctor Strange, we see
Kaecilius and his minions get sucked into

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.360
the Dark Dimension, losing all their features
in the process … and looking suspiciously

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.850
like Mindless Ones during their transformation,
with glints of light replacing their faces.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.990
It seems likely that anyone who allies with
Dormammu and his promise of eternal life will

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.100
eventually be transformed into a Mindless
One.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.090
What this means for the Ancient One?

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.930
We may never know…

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.850
The Doors of Perception

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:04.880
The obligatory Stan Lee cameo was so much
more than just an old dude reading on the

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:05.880
bus.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.140
The book that Lee is giggling at is The Doors
of Perception by Aldous Huxley.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:13.390
Not only is it a literal reference to Strange's
perception of reality being forced open, but

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:17.850
the book is pretty much an essay about a psychedelic
drug trip, which is no small comment on both

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.660
the dazzling special effects of the movie,
and the Dark Dimension.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.890
That place looks like a Steve Ditko drawing
come to life, and it's practically a spitting

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:29.060
image of those 1970s Third Eye blacklight
posters Marvel used to sell for your parents'

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.710
teenage drug dens.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:31.710
"Ohhhh!"

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.510
"Wow, man!"

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:34.510
Thanks for watching!

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:37.500
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel and watch more videos like

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.700
the one you just saw.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.460
And don't forget to check out all this cool
